body:
As I said, it is signal's name. Actually, I went ahead and added a `print_line` to check it:

```c++
bool Object::_disconnect_all(const StringName &p_signal, bool p_force) {
	// use p_force like in _disconnect
	OBJ_SIGNAL_LOCK

        // >>> HERE WE ARE PRINTING WHAT `p_signal` IS <<<
	print_line(vformat("Disconnecting all connections from signal '%s' in '%s'.", p_signal, to_string()));

	SignalData *s = signal_map.getptr(p_signal);
	if (!s) {
		bool signal_is_valid = ClassDB::has_signal(get_class_name(), p_signal) ||
				(script_instance && script_instance->get_script()->has_script_signal(p_signal));
		ERR_FAIL_COND_V_MSG(signal_is_valid, false, vformat("Attempt to disconnect all connections from nonexistent signal '%s'. Signal: '%s'.", to_string(), p_signal));
	}
	ERR_FAIL_NULL_V_MSG(s, false, vformat("Disconnecting all connections from nonexistent signal '%s' in '%s'.", p_signal, to_string()));
	ERR_FAIL_COND_V_MSG(s->slot_map.is_empty(), false, vformat("No connections to disconnect from signal '%s' in '%s'.", p_signal, to_string()));
	s->slot_map.clear();
	if (ClassDB::has_signal(get_class_name(), p_signal) && s->user.name.is_empty()) {
		//not user signal, delete
		signal_map.erase(p_signal);
	}
	return true;
}
```

Then, build:

```
scons platform=linuxbsd dev_mode=yes dev_build=yes
```

Then, import and run the sample project I submitted in PR. Just in case, I will give GDScript to test it out as well (it's already available in the sample project in the post):

```gdscript
extends Control

var has_conn = null
var con_len = null

func _ready() -> void:
	$Button.connect("pressed", func():
		print("First signal")
	)
	$Button.connect("pressed", func():
		print("Second signal")
	)
	
	has_conn = $Button.pressed.has_connections()
	con_len = $Button.pressed.get_connections().size()
	print("has connections? ", has_conn)
	print("connection count? ", con_len)
	
	$Button.pressed.disconnect_all()
	
	has_conn = $Button.pressed.has_connections()
	con_len = $Button.pressed.get_connections().size()
	print("has connections? ", has_conn)
	print("connection count? ", con_len)
```

...which prints out:

```plain
has connections? true
connection count? 2
Disconnecting all connections from signal 'pressed' in 'Button:<Button#27330086361>'.
has connections? false
connection count? 0
```

The "pressed" in `Disconnecting all connections ...` line comes from `print_line` in C++ side, where `p_signal` == `"pressed"`.

***

The reason for its existence is because *we need to know **which exact signal we need** to disconnect all callables from*, because `Button` has a lot of signals:

<img width="279" height="769" alt="image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/bc680b98-3538-41e4-b0fb-c09c421f235f" />

In the GDScript sample, I tested it via `pressed` signal, which could be another one, like `button_down` or `button_up` etc.

Without `p_signal`, how could we discern which signal we are disconnecting all callables from?
